---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Linkage Disequilibrium, Recombination Hotspots & Chromosomal Haplotype Architecture";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical and biological exploration of Linkage Disequilibrium (D, D', r²), exact exponential half-life decay under recombination, PRDM9 zinc-finger sequence motifs, SPO11 double-strand breaks, and human haplotype block structure.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 903 prose words, 7 display and 80 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "10 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 2</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Mathematical</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      Why are certain combinations of genetic alleles inherited together across thousands of generations, while others are swiftly scrambled by meiotic recombination? How does a single zinc-finger histone methyltransferase (PRDM9) dictate the architectural landscape of human genetic variation? This deep dive explores <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong> from first principles: the mathematical formulations of <Katex tex="D" />, <Katex tex="D'" />, and <Katex tex="r^2" />, the exact generational decay dynamics under meiotic crossing over, Haldane and Kosambi mapping functions, PRDM9 chromatin recruitment of SPO11, and the resulting structure of <strong>human haplotype blocks</strong>.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#gametic-phase">1. Gametic Phase & The 2×2 Haplotype Contingency Table</a></li>
        <li><a href="#metrics">2. Mathematical Metrics: D, Lewontin's D', and Hill-Robertson r²</a></li>
        <li><a href="#decay">3. Generational Decay & Exact Half-Life of LD</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mapping">4. Genetic Mapping Functions: Haldane vs. Kosambi</a></li>
        <li><a href="#prdm9">5. Molecular Biology of Recombination: PRDM9 & SPO11</a></li>
        <li><a href="#blocks">6. Haplotype Blocks, Tag SNPs & Imputation Tagability</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="gametic-phase">
      <h2>1. Gametic Phase & The 2×2 Haplotype Contingency Table</h2>
      <p>
        Consider two biallelic loci on the same chromosome: Locus 1 with alleles <Katex tex="A" /> (frequency <Katex tex="p_A" />) and <Katex tex="a" /> (frequency <Katex tex="p_a = 1 - p_A" />), and Locus 2 with alleles <Katex tex="B" /> (frequency <Katex tex="p_B" />) and <Katex tex="b" /> (frequency <Katex tex="p_b = 1 - p_B" />).
      </p>
      <p>
        In diploid organisms, there are four possible gametic combinations (haplotypes): <Katex tex="AB, Ab, aB, ab" /> with population frequencies <Katex tex="p_{AB}, p_{Ab}, p_{aB}, p_{ab}" />:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Allele</th>
              <th>Allele <Katex tex="B" /></th>
              <th>Allele <Katex tex="b" /></th>
              <th>Marginal Frequency</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Allele <Katex tex="A" /></strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_{AB}" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_{Ab}" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_A = p_{AB} + p_{Ab}" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Allele <Katex tex="a" /></strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_{aB}" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_{ab}" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_a = p_{aB} + p_{ab}" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Marginal Frequency</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_B = p_{AB} + p_{aB}" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_b = p_{Ab} + p_{ab}" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1.0" /></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        If alleles segregate completely independently (linkage equilibrium), gamete frequencies equal the product of marginal allele frequencies: <Katex tex="p_{AB} = p_A p_B" />. Any departure from independence constitutes <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="metrics">
      <h2>2. Mathematical Metrics: D, Lewontin's D', and Hill-Robertson r²</h2>

      <h3>1. The Raw LD Coefficient (D)</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Linkage Disequilibrium Coefficient (Robbins 1918)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = p_{AB} - p_A p_B = p_{AB} p_{ab} - p_{Ab} p_{aB}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="D = 0" />: Complete linkage equilibrium &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="D > 0" />: Alleles <Katex tex="A" /> and <Katex tex="B" /> co-occur more frequently than expected by chance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="D < 0" />: Alleles <Katex tex="A" /> and <Katex tex="B" /> repel each other.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>2. Lewontin's Normalized D' (1964)</h3>
      <p>
        The raw value of <Katex tex="D" /> is constrained by marginal allele frequencies (<Katex tex="D_{\min} \le D \le D_{\max}" />). Richard Lewontin (1964) normalized <Katex tex="D" /> by its theoretical maximum given the marginal frequencies:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Lewontin's Normalized LD Coefficient</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D' = \frac{D}{D_{\max}}, \qquad D_{\max} = \begin{cases} \min(p_A p_b, p_a p_B) & \text{if } D > 0 \\ \min(p_A p_B, p_a p_b) & \text{if } D < 0 \end{cases}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="|D'| = 1" /> indicates <strong>complete LD</strong>: at least one of the 4 possible haplotypes is completely absent from the population, proving that <strong>no historical recombination</strong> has occurred between the loci since the mutation arose.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>3. Squared Correlation Coefficient r² (Hill & Robertson 1968)</h3>
      <p>
        In statistical genetics and GWAS, the squared Pearson correlation coefficient <Katex tex="r^2" /> is the gold-standard metric:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Hill-Robertson Correlation Coefficient</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="r^2 = \frac{D^2}{p_A (1 - p_A) p_B (1 - p_B)} = \frac{(p_{AB} p_{ab} - p_{Ab} p_{aB})^2}{p_A p_a p_B p_b}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="r^2 = 1" /> indicates <strong>perfect LD</strong>: alleles <Katex tex="A" /> and <Katex tex="B" /> are completely redundant (<Katex tex="p_A = p_B" /> and <Katex tex="p_{AB} = p_A" />).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> D' vs. r²: Distinct Biological Meanings
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong><Katex tex="D' = 1" /> (Historical Recombination):</strong> Means no crossover has broken the ancestral chromosome. However, if allele frequencies differ (e.g. <Katex tex="p_A = 0.5" /> and <Katex tex="p_B = 0.01" />), <Katex tex="r^2" /> will be near zero (<Katex tex="r^2 \approx 0.02" />).
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong><Katex tex="r^2" /> (Tagging & Statistical Power):</strong> Quantifies sample size efficiency in GWAS: if a causal variant has correlation <Katex tex="r^2" /> with a genotyped tag SNP, testing the tag SNP requires <Katex tex="N / r^2" /> samples to achieve the same statistical power as testing the causal variant directly (Pritchard & Przeworski 2001).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="decay">
      <h2>3. Generational Decay & Exact Half-Life of LD</h2>
      <p>
        During meiosis, crossover events between homologous chromosomes recombine maternal and paternal alleles at rate <Katex tex="\theta \in [0, 0.5]" /> per generation.
      </p>

      <h3>Generational Recurrence Relation</h3>
      <p>
        In generation <Katex tex="t+1" />, a proportion <Katex tex="(1 - \theta)" /> of gametes are non-recombinant (inheriting intact haplotypes), while <Katex tex="\theta" /> are recombinant:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Generational Decay of Linkage Disequilibrium</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="p_{AB}^{(t+1)} = (1 - \theta) p_{AB}^{(t)} + \theta p_A p_B \implies D_{t+1} = (1 - \theta) D_t \implies D_t = D_0 (1 - \theta)^t \approx D_0 e^{-\theta t}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Exact Half-Life of LD</h3>
      <p>
        The number of generations <Katex tex="t_{1/2}" /> required for LD to decrease to 50% of its initial value (<Katex tex="D_t / D_0 = 0.5" />) is:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Exact LD Half-Life Scaling (P0 Formulation)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="t_{1/2} = \frac{\ln(0.5)}{\ln(1 - \theta)} \approx \frac{0.693}{\theta} \quad (\text{dimensionless fraction } \theta) \approx \frac{69.3}{d} \quad (\text{genetic distance } d \text{ in cM})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          For markers separated by <Katex tex="1 \text{ cM} \approx \theta = 0.01" />, the half-life of LD is <Katex tex="t_{1/2} \approx 69" /> generations (<Katex tex="\approx 2,000" /> years in humans).
          For closely linked markers separated by <Katex tex="10 \text{ kb}" /> (<Katex tex="\theta \approx 10^{-4}" />), <Katex tex="t_{1/2} \approx 6,930" /> generations (<Katex tex="\approx 200,000" /> years).
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="mapping">
      <h2>4. Genetic Mapping Functions: Haldane vs. Kosambi</h2>
      <p>
        Recombination fraction <Katex tex="\theta" /> is non-additive across chromosomal distances because multiple crossovers cancel out in pairs. <strong>Genetic distance</strong> <Katex tex="d" /> (measured in Morgans or centiMorgans, where 1 Morgan = 1 expected crossover per meiosis) is converted to <Katex tex="\theta" /> via mapping functions:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Haldane (1919) vs. Kosambi (1944) Mapping Functions</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{Haldane (No Interference): } d = -\frac{1}{2} \ln(1 - 2\theta) \iff \theta = \frac{1}{2}\left(1 - e^{-2d}\right)" display={true} />
          <Katex tex="\text{Kosambi (Crossover Interference): } d = \frac{1}{4} \ln\left(\frac{1 + 2\theta}{1 - 2\theta}\right) \iff \theta = \frac{1}{2} \tanh(2d)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Haldane assumes crossovers follow a spatial Poisson process with zero interference. Kosambi incorporates positive crossover interference (the physical occurrence of one chiasma reduces the probability of a nearby second chiasma).
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="prdm9">
      <h2>5. Molecular Biology of Recombination: PRDM9 & SPO11</h2>
      <p>
        In mammals, meiotic crossing over is strictly non-random. Over 80% of human recombination events are concentrated in narrow 1–2 kb <strong>recombination hotspots</strong> dictated by the zinc-finger protein <strong>PRDM9</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="activity" size={15} /> The Molecular Cascade of Recombination Hotspots
        </div>
        <p>
          1. <strong>Sequence Recognition:</strong> PRDM9 uses its rapidly evolving C2H2 zinc-finger array to bind a degenerate 13-bp DNA motif (<Katex tex="\text{CCNCCNTNNCCNC}" />).
          <br/>
          2. <strong>Epigenetic Activation:</strong> PRDM9's SET domain deposits dual histone marks: <strong>H3K4me3</strong> (histone H3 lysine-4 trimethylation) and <strong>H3K36me3</strong> on flanking nucleosomes.
          <br/>
          3. <strong>Programmed DSB Induction:</strong> Topoisomerase-like transesterase <strong>SPO11</strong> is recruited to cleave chromosomal DNA, generating double-strand breaks (DSBs).
          <br/>
          4. <strong>Homology Search & Repair:</strong> DSBs are processed by RAD51 and DMC1 for homologous strand invasion, resolving into either <strong>crossovers</strong> (reciprocal exchange creating LD breakdown) or <strong>non-crossover gene conversions</strong> (local patch repair without flanking exchange).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="blocks">
      <h2>6. Haplotype Blocks, Tag SNPs & Imputation Tagability</h2>
      <p>
        Because recombination is punctate, the human genome is structured into discrete <strong>haplotype blocks</strong>—genomic segments of 10–100 kb with minimal internal recombination and high Linkage Disequilibrium (<Katex tex="r^2 > 0.8" />), punctuated by sharp recombination hotspots.
      </p>

      <h3>Gabriel et al. (2002) Confidence Interval Definition</h3>
      <p>
        The landmark HapMap Project defined haplotype blocks using 90% confidence bounds on <Katex tex="D'" />:
        <br/>
        &bull; A marker pair is in <strong>strong LD</strong> if the one-sided upper 95% confidence bound on <Katex tex="D' \ge 0.98" /> and lower bound <Katex tex="\ge 0.70" />.
        <br/>
        &bull; A block is a contiguous segment where <Katex tex="\ge 95\%" /> of informative marker pairs exhibit strong LD.
      </p>

      <h3>Tag SNPs and GWAS Genotyping Arrays</h3>
      <p>
        Within a haplotype block of 50 SNPs, high LD means only 3–5 common haplotypes exist. Genotyping a small subset of <strong>tag SNPs</strong> allows statistical reconstruction of all remaining unmeasured variants via <strong>Hidden Markov Model (HMM) imputation</strong> (e.g. Beagle, Minimac4, IMPUTE5).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>LD Metrics:</strong> <Katex tex="D" /> measures raw gametic covariance; Lewontin's <Katex tex="D'" /> reflects historical recombination absence; <Katex tex="r^2" /> dictates GWAS tagging power.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Decay Half-Life:</strong> <Katex tex="t_{1/2} = \frac{\ln(0.5)}{\ln(1 - \theta)} \approx \frac{0.693}{\theta}" /> for fractional <Katex tex="\theta" />, or <Katex tex="t_{1/2} \approx \frac{69.3}{d}" /> for distance in cM.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>PRDM9 Hotspots:</strong> Zinc-finger DNA binding + H3K4me3/H3K36me3 chromatin marking recruits SPO11, confining recombination to narrow 1–2 kb hotspots.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: Heritability Estimation & GREML (GCTA)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 3 to explore the missing heritability paradox, the realized Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM), and Yang et al. (2010) GCTA variance component estimation via AI-REML.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-heritability-greml/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 3: Heritability & GREML</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
